Date: Wed, 11 Feb 2027 09:18:00 -0800
Subject: Re: BlueCrest integration ops sprint — SDK setup, endpoint cutover & retry plan

Hey Liam — quick technical notes to address your infra questions and add a compact retry helper that your wrapper can re-use.

1) SDK version: Use @redwoodai/sdk@2.3.x (patch is backward compatible with 2.1.0 but includes better streaming reconnect semantics and explicit rate-limit parsing). We'll open a trivial PR to your wrapper to bump if you want.

2) Streaming: you should NOT blindly retry the entire streaming request. We recommend checkpointing the last confirmed token id on your side and using Redwood's resumed-stream API (v2 supports chunk resume via resume_after_token_id). If you cannot resume, fall back to a short-window re-generation with strict max_tokens to avoid excess cost.

3) Minimal retry helper (Node) — drop into your wrapper (idempotent-check omitted for clarity):

async function withRetries(fn, opts = {maxAttempts: 3, baseMs: 300}) {
  const max = opts.maxAttempts;
  let attempt = 0;
  while (attempt < max) {
    try {
      return await fn();
    } catch (err) {
      attempt++;
      const retriable = err.status === 429 || (err.code >= 500 && err.code < 600);
      if (!retriable || attempt >= max) throw err;
      const jitter = Math.floor(Math.random() * 200) - 100;
      const delay = Math.pow(2, attempt - 1) * opts.baseMs + jitter;
      await new Promise(r => setTimeout(r, delay));
    }
  }
}

4) Endpoint migration detail: When routing to v2 during staged cutover, we will return a special header X-Redwood-Variant: v2 or legacy. Please log that header for parity checks. Also we will add a response header X-RateLimit-Policy which enumerates the quota bucket to help you tune local throttles.

I'll join the call tomorrow to walk through the PR and run an end-to-end smoke.

- Marcus

